<node name="/" xmlns:doc="http://www.freedesktop.org/dbus/1.0/doc.dtd">
  <!--
      org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.WebAuthentication1:
      @short_description: Portal for completing a web sign-in flow

      This interface lets applications run web sign-in flows in a web view
      controlled by the desktop. The application supplies the start and
      completion URIs and receives the matching completion URI. Cookies and
      redirects, as well as client certificate prompts and PIN entry, are
      handled outside the application. The application has no access to the
      web view.

      The portal validates the returned URI against the requested completion
      URI before returning it to the application. The web view is restricted
      to sign-in flows.

      This is an experimental interface and can change or get removed at
      any time. An incompatible change increments the number at the end of
      the interface name. See :doc:`experimental-portals`.

      This documentation describes version 1 of this interface.
  -->
  <interface name="org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.WebAuthentication1">
    <!--
        Start:
        @parent_window: Identifier for the application window, see :doc:`window-identifiers`
        @start_uri: The URI to open
        @completion_uri: The URI whose navigation ends the flow
        @options: Vardict with optional further information
        @handle: Object path for the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` object representing this call

        Starts a web sign-in flow.

        Sandboxed applications require network access to call this method.
        The call fails with
        ``org.freedesktop.portal.Error.NotAllowed``.

        @start_uri must be an absolute ``https`` URI with a host.

        @completion_uri must be an absolute URI with no userinfo. An ``http``
        or ``https`` completion URI must name a host. A private-use scheme
        redirect URI as described in :rfc:`8252` section 7.1, such as
        ``com.example.app:/oauth2redirect``, has no authority and is matched
        on its scheme and path. A completion URI must have a host or a path.

        A host containing ``*`` is rejected with a D-Bus error.

        @completion_uri is matched against every navigation the web view is
        asked to make in any frame. Both URIs are parsed before their
        components are compared:

        * the scheme and the host are compared case insensitively, and a URI
          with a host never matches one without;
        * the port is compared with the default port of the scheme filled in,
          so ``https://example.com/x`` and ``https://example.com:443/x``
          are the same URI, and a private-use scheme has no default port;
        * the path is compared exactly, on the form GLib's URI parser
          produces;
        * the query and the fragment carry the result of the flow and take no
          part in matching.

        Parsing normalises paths and hosts:

        * Unreserved escapes are decoded, so ``/%63allback`` matches
          ``/callback``.
        * Other escapes use uppercase hex, so ``/call%2fback`` matches
          ``/call%2Fback``.
        * Dot segments are resolved, so ``/a/../callback`` and ``/./callback``
          both match ``/callback``.
        * Internationalised hosts are converted to punycode and match their
          punycode spelling.

        An empty path differs from ``/``, so ``https://example.com`` does not
        match ``https://example.com/``. A host with a trailing dot differs
        from the same host without it.

        A matching navigation ends the flow and must never be loaded. This
        also applies to subframes. Backends must apply the same rule to every
        frame.

        Malformed requests return a D-Bus error before the backend is called
        or a window is opened.

        Supported keys in the @options vardict include:

        * ``handle_token`` (``s``)

          A string that will be used as the last element of the @handle. Must be a valid
          object path element. See the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` documentation for
          more information about the @handle.

        * ``activation_token`` (``s``)

          A token that can be used to activate the window, see
          :doc:`window-identifiers`.

        * ``session_mode`` (``s``)

          Web storage mode: ``shared`` (the default) or ``ephemeral``.
          ``shared`` storage is shared between flows run by this backend and
          isolated from the user's browser. ``ephemeral`` storage is created
          for this flow and destroyed when it ends. Unknown values are
          rejected. This interface creates no
          :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Session` object.

        * ``timeout`` (``u``)

          Maximum duration of the flow in seconds. The default is 300 and
          values above 900 are clamped to 900. When the deadline expires, the
          portal closes the backend request to dismiss its window and returns
          response ``2`` with ``reason`` ``timeout``. The timeout is also
          passed to the backend, which may end the flow sooner. Applications
          can cancel the flow by calling
          :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request.Close`.

        * ``title`` (``s``)

          A short description of the sign-in purpose, displayed as
          application-supplied text. Backends must not use it as the window's
          identity.

        The following results get returned via the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request::Response` signal:

        * ``completion_uri`` (``s``)

          The matched URI, complete and undecoded, including query and
          fragment. Present only when the response is ``0``. The portal
          validates this value against @completion_uri before returning it.
          If it does not match, the response is ``2`` with
          ``reason`` ``backend_completion_mismatch`` and the URI is discarded.

        * ``reason`` (``s``)

          An optional stable symbol explaining a non-zero response. The portal
          emits ``timeout``, ``backend_disappeared``,
          ``backend_completion_mismatch`` and ``backend_protocol_error``, and
          forwards backend values such as ``timeout``,
          ``no_display``, ``user_cancelled`` or ``credential_unavailable``. It
          provides diagnostic information. Consumers must use the response
          code and tolerate missing or unknown reasons.
    -->
    <method name="Start">
      <arg type="s" name="parent_window" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="start_uri" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="completion_uri" direction="in"/>
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In3" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="o" name="handle" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <property name="version" type="u" access="read"/>
  </interface>
</node>
